WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.880
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:12.560
Today we are going to see what DeepMind’s amazing&nbsp;
AI can learn from humans doing silly things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:16.240
like playing the drums with a comb.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:21.840
Really, we have to do that? Well…okay,&nbsp;
if you insist. There you go.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:27.200
And? Now what? Well, if it had seen&nbsp;
enough of these instruction/video pairs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:33.840
hopefully it will be able to learn from it. And&nbsp;
here comes the fundamental question we are here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:42.480
to answer today. If this AI does something, is&nbsp;
this imitation, or is this learning? Imitation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:48.480
is not that impressive, but if it can learn&nbsp;
general concepts from examples like this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:55.280
now that would perhaps be a little spark of&nbsp;
intelligence. That is what we are looking for.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:00.000
So, how much did it learn?&nbsp;
Well, let’s see together.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:08.000
Nice, look at that! If we ask it, it can&nbsp;
now show us where a specific room is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:16.480
And if we get a little lost, it even helps us with&nbsp;
additional instructions to make sure we find out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:23.920
way into the bathroom. Now note that the layout&nbsp;
of this virtual playhouse is randomized each time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:31.360
we start a new interaction, therefore it cannot&nbsp;
just imitate the direction people go when they say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:37.360
bathroom. It doesn’t work, because&nbsp;
next time, it will be elsewhere. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:43.440
actually has to understand what a&nbsp;
bathroom is. Good job, little AI!

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:51.280
It also understands that we are holding a bucket&nbsp;
and it should put the grapes in that. Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:58.400
so it learned from instructions, and it can deal&nbsp;
with instructions. But if it had learned general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:04.960
concepts, it should be able to do other things&nbsp;
too! You know what, let’s ask some questions.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:08.640
How many grapes are there in this scene?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:18.960
2. Yes, that is correct, good job! Now, are they&nbsp;
the same color or different? It says different.&nbsp;

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:27.040
I like it, because to answer this question, it has&nbsp;
to remember what we just talked about. Very good!

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:33.040
And now, hold on to your papers,&nbsp;
and check this out! We ask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:40.560
what color the grapes are on the floor.&nbsp;
And what does it do? It understands what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:47.440
it needs to look for to gather the required&nbsp;
information. Yes, it uses its little peepers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:53.600
and answers the question. A little&nbsp;
virtual assistant AI! I love it.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:03:00.160
But, it isn’t just an assistant. Here come&nbsp;
two of my favorite tasks from the paper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:07.920
One, if we dreamed up a catchy tune and we&nbsp;
just need a bandmate to make it happen. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:13.200
say no more! This little AI is&nbsp;
right there to help. So cool!

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:21.760
And it gets better, get this, two, it even knows&nbsp;
about its limitations. For instance, it knows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:29.200
that it can’t flip the green box over. This can&nbsp;
only arise from knowledge and experimentation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:35.040
with the physics of this virtual world. This&nbsp;
will be fantastic for testing video games.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:43.200
So, yes, the learning is indeed thorough. Now&nbsp;
let’s answer two more super important questions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:49.760
One, how quickly does this AI&nbsp;
learn, and is it as good as a human?

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:58.320
So, learning speed. Let’s look under the&nbsp;
hood together, and - oh yes, yes, YES!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:06.640
I am super happy. You are asking, Károly, why are&nbsp;
you super happy? I am super happy because learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:14.960
is already happening by watching humans mingle&nbsp;
for about 12 minutes. That is next to nothing!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:23.280
Amazing. And, one more thing that makes me perhaps&nbsp;
even happier. And that is, that I don’t see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:29.760
a sudden spike in the growth, I see nice,&nbsp;
linear growth instead. Why is that important?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:38.000
Well, this means there is a higher chance that the&nbsp;
algorithm is slowly learning general concepts, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:45.600
starts understanding new things that we might ask&nbsp;
it. And if it were a small spike, there would be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:52.560
higher chance that it had seen something similar&nbsp;
to a human clearing a table in the training set,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.560 --> 00:05:00.000
and suddenly started copying it. But, with this&nbsp;
kind of growth, there is less of a chance that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:08.080
is just copying the teacher. I love it. And, yes&nbsp;
this is the corner of the internet where we get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:16.080
unreasonably excited by a blue line. Welcome to&nbsp;
