00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:13.760
But actually, one more question: A virtual&nbsp;
assistant is pretty cool, but why is this so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:20.160
important? What does this have to do with our&nbsp;
lives? Well, have a look at how OpenAI trained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:27.200
their robot hand in a simulation to be able to&nbsp;
rotate these Rubik cubes. And then, deployed this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:35.600
software onto a real robot hand, and, look! It can&nbsp;
use this simulation knowledge, and now it works in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:43.520
the real world too. But sim2real has relevance&nbsp;
to self-driving cars too. Look. Tesla is already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:50.640
working on creating virtual worlds, and training&nbsp;
their cars there. One of the advantages of that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:58.160
that we can create really unlikely and potentially&nbsp;
unsafe scenarios, but, in these virtual worlds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:04.880
the self-driving AI can train itself safely.&nbsp;
And when they deploy them into the real world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:10.160
they will have all this knowledge. Waymo&nbsp;
is running similar experiments too.

